WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.490 --> 00:00:07.770
In the near future, cash could become
obsolete, and your phone could completely

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:12.030
replace your wallet. Mobile payments,
which are already commonplace in

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:16.529
numerous countries worldwide, are
steadily gaining traction. In China's

00:00:16.529 --> 00:00:21.689
biggest cities, ninety-two percent of people use WeChat Pay or Alipay, the market's main

00:00:21.689 --> 00:00:27.029
competitors, and mobile payments are
accepted by restaurants, retailers, street

00:00:27.029 --> 00:00:33.960
vendors, and even taxis. More broadly, in
2018, the mobile money industry completed

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:39.300
1.3 billion dollars of transactions
daily across ninety countries. This

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:43.860
underscores a shift toward a cashless
society dominated by electronic banking

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:47.640
and digital finance, where physical
transactions like bartering are

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:53.070
virtually non-existent. Proponents for a
cashless system believe that digital

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:57.629
money increases convenience and could
reduce robbery and counterfeiting, while

00:00:57.629 --> 00:01:01.680
opponents argue that it endangers
privacy and hurts disadvantaged

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:06.270
communities that rely on cash. And while
many of the long-term consequences of

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:09.920
cashlessness remain to be seen, the
movement towards a cashless society

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:15.260
seems highly likely, if not inevitable.

